WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.763
This lesson covers Scratchpad files, and although it's late in the course, it is critically

2
00:00:05.763 --> 00:00:08.676
important to understanding how Clodd operates.

3
00:00:08.739 --> 00:00:16.340
Every new Clodd code session opens with zero memory of past work. A Scratchpad file is the repo that solves that.

4
00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:22.800
We'll look at the problem, what to put in the file, what to keep out, and how to enforce the habit

5
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.174
of reading and writing consistently.

6
00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:27.000
Let's get into it.

7
00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:34.967
A Scratchpad file is just a plain text file in the repo, typically named notes.md or context.md, and

8
00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:40.222
it is the entire mechanism for carrying work across a context reset.

9
00:00:40.299 --> 00:00:46.900
What it holds is cross-session knowledge, the category of information that should survive that reset.

10
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:52.959
Decisions you have made, dead ends you have already explored, naming conventions your team has agreed on.

11
00:00:52.959 --> 00:00:59.659
Inside it, a decision log is a dedicated subsection for architectural choices and their rationale.

12
00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:05.959
The decision log matters most because without it, a future session will re-examine a question you already resolved.

13
00:01:05.959 --> 00:01:11.781
All three of these are practical tools, not just vocabulary, and together they define what a

14
00:01:11.781 --> 00:01:13.996
Scratchpad is trying to accomplish.

15
00:01:14.059 --> 00:01:20.360
Here is the core problem. When a new Clodd code session starts, the context window is completely blank.

16
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.169
The agent has no memory of what happened in the previous session. That sounds like a minor

17
00:01:25.169 --> 00:01:27.306
inconvenience, but it compounds quickly.

18
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.760
The agent will re-explore dead ends you already ruled out. It will re-ask questions you already answered.

19
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.760
It will make naming choices that contradict decisions from earlier sessions.

20
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:44.126
Every session effectively starts over, which means you are paying the cost of discovery work

21
00:01:44.126 --> 00:01:45.701
repeatedly instead of once.

22
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.452
The Scratchpad file is the practical fix for this. It is a lightweight mechanism to carry knowledge

23
00:01:51.452 --> 00:01:54.902
forward across the boundary that the context window creates.

24
00:01:54.959 --> 00:02:01.360
The cost of maintaining a Scratchpad is low. The cost of not having one grows with every session.

25
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:08.160
Think of it as two workflows, side by side. In the no-Scratchpad workflow, each session starts from scratch.

26
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.592
The agent doesn't know about the dead ends, doesn't remember the agreed naming conventions, and may

27
00:02:13.592 --> 00:02:17.105
contradict earlier architectural decisions without realizing it.

28
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:23.099
That drift accumulates across a long project. In the with-Scratchpad workflow, the agent reads the

29
00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:25.099
file at the start of the session,

30
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:31.660
picks up current task state, respects recorded decisions, and avoids already explored dead ends.

31
00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:37.710
Before the session closes, it writes updates. The gap between those two workflows gets wider the

32
00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:39.096
longer a project runs.

33
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:45.453
A single day of work without a Scratchpad is manageable. A week or a month of work without one is

34
00:02:45.453 --> 00:02:47.595
genuinely painful to reconstruct.

35
00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:52.660
What belongs in the Scratchpad? Think of it as the evolving layer of your project.

36
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:59.660
The Scratchpad holds things that change run-to-run. First, architectural decisions and the reasoning behind them.

37
00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:05.641
If you decided to use a particular data structure, a particular API shape, or a particular file

38
00:03:05.641 --> 00:03:08.097
layout, record that choice and the why.

39
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.983
Second, dead ends. If you tried an approach and it didn't work, write that down so the next session

40
00:03:13.983 --> 00:03:16.101
doesn't waste time on the same path.

41
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:22.660
Third, naming conventions. If a term has a specific meaning in this codebase, state it.

42
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:28.660
Fourth, current task state. What is in progress? What is blocked? What the next action is?

43
00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:33.660
These four categories cover most of what persistence actually needs to capture.

44
00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:39.962
Just as important as knowing what to include is knowing what to leave out. Three things don't belong

45
00:03:39.962 --> 00:03:41.096
in the Scratchpad.

46
00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:48.113
First, code. The codebase is where code lives. Copying snippets into the Scratchpad creates a second

47
00:03:48.113 --> 00:03:51.590
source that drifts from the actual implementation.

48
00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.660
If you need to reference code, point to the file, not the content.

49
00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:02.240
Second, credentials. Secrets, tokens, and API keys should never appear in a plain text file that

50
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.091
gets committed to the repo.

51
00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.660
That is a security boundary, not a style preference.

52
00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:14.660
Third, private user data. Any personal or sensitive information has no place in a shared Scratchpad.

53
00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:22.660
Keeping the Scratchpad focused on workflow state, not content, also makes it easier to prune and maintain over time.

54
00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:28.660
Reading the Scratchpad at session start sounds obvious, but it requires explicit enforcement.

55
00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:34.160
Left to default behavior, an agent won't automatically seek out a file you haven't told it about.

56
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:41.660
The most reliable approach is to list the Scratchpad in clod.md as one of the first files to read at session start.

57
00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.160
That puts the instruction in a place the agent checks reliably.

58
00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:52.160
A more automated approach is a session start hook that surfaces the file before any user message is processed.

59
00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.660
Either approach works. What doesn't work is assuming the agent will find and read the file on its own.

60
00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:04.660
The read has to be mandatory, not optional, and the mechanism has to enforce that consistently every session.

61
00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:08.660
The write half of the discipline is as important as the read half.

62
00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:13.660
Before you close a session, the agent should update the Scratchpad to reflect what happened.

63
00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:18.660
That means recording decisions made during the session, noting any dead ends reached,

64
00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.660
and updating the current task state block so the next session knows where to pick up.

65
00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:29.156
A session end hook runs on termination for cleanup, but it can't block, so the write stays

66
00:05:29.156 --> 00:05:31.598
instruction-driven and depends on habit.

67
00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:37.160
One detail worth emphasizing is that a stale Scratchpad is worse than no Scratchpad.

68
00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:43.097
If the agent reads an outdated file and trusts what it says, it will act on information that no

69
00:05:43.097 --> 00:05:44.597
longer reflects reality.

70
00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:49.660
The read and the write are a pair, and both have to happen reliably.

71
00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:54.160
The Scratchpad puts memory in the repo instead of inside the model.

72
00:05:54.160 --> 00:06:00.660
The habit that makes it pay off is writing the file for the next session, not for yourself.

73
00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:06.408
If it doesn't say what got decided and what was already ruled out, tomorrow's session starts cold

74
00:06:06.408 --> 00:06:08.600
and quietly re-does all of your work.

75
00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:12.660
That closes out this session. I'll see you in the next one.

